# The zero pipeline's declared delta: what each phase changes, in advance.
#
# Read by tools/zerodelta.sh --phase N, which takes every line up to phase N and
# checks the binary moved in exactly that way -- and nothing else (ZERO-GOAL.md,
# rule 2).  The grammar is pipes/whim.delta's, one line per phase that changes the
# list, continuation lines indented:
#
#   word             an Ex command name: its block in ref-excmds.txt now differs
#   case:NAME        a screen case (tools/zcases.py) whose record now differs
#   argv:NAME        an invocation (tools/zargv.py); spaces are written as `_`
#   term-moved       the terminal table (tools/ztermcheck.py) now differs
#   pty-moved        the pty scenarios (tools/zpty.py) now differ
#   screen-moved     what the editor DRAWS differs everywhere -- the snapshots and
#                    the stream digest of every case, the text and message lines of
#                    every command row.  Exit status, bells and stderr must still
#                    match, case by case
#   stderr-moved     what the editor writes to STDERR differs everywhere, and
#                    nothing else does
#   drop:X           X was declared earlier and no longer differs
#
# The two `-moved` dimensions are not a way of saying "some things changed".  Each
# excludes ONE dimension from every comparison and must itself have moved
# somewhere: a phase that declares `screen-moved` and draws the same screens fails
# (proven, by declaring it here and watching tools/zcompare.py refuse).  Everything
# outside the declared dimension is still checked record by record.
#
# THE DIFFERENCE IS FROM WHIM-VIM, not from slim-vim.  The baselines it is measured
# against are .reference/zero-baselines, which zero phase 0 records from the
# committed whim-vim.c built with whim's own compile line.  So everything whim
# removed is already in them, and the list below starts empty: each zero phase
# declares only what it changes relative to whim.
#
# 0 declares nothing: the seed is whim-vim.c byte for byte, built -no-pie, and the
# harnesses must see no difference at all.
#
# 1 declares nothing: -fno-stack-protector changes the code gcc emits around a
# function's locals, not what the editor does.
#
# 2 removes the two warnings check_tty() printed when stdin or stdout was not a
# terminal, the two seconds it then paused, and --ttyfail.  IT DECLARED NOTHING
# UNTIL PHASE 3, and that was true of the old instrument rather than of the
# editor: behaviour.py and exsweep.py ran it `-e -s`, where exmode_active is set
# before check_tty() and its other branch is taken, so no recorded stderr ever
# held the warnings, and termcheck.py drives a pty where both streams ARE
# terminals.  The instrument phase 3 installs runs the editor on a pipe, which is
# exactly where those warnings were printed, so the difference is visible now and
# is declared where it happened: every one of the 102 cases, 109 of the 111 command
# rows (:stop and :suspend are skipped and run nothing) and 13 of the 30 command
# lines differ in their stderr and in nothing else -- measured with
# tools/zcompare.py, which is what "and in nothing else" means here.
2     stderr-moved
#
# 3 declares nothing of its own.  It changes no source: it replaces the
# instrument, and the line above is the difference that instrument can now see.
#
# 4 removes Ex mode, silent mode and the four options that reach them.  Six records
# move and every one of them is a way IN to Ex mode: the two keys, and the three
# command lines the parser accepted for it.  `key_Q` and `key_gQ` drew "Entering Ex
# mode.  Type \"visual\" to go to Normal mode." and now beep once, from nv_error and
# from nv_g_cmd's default; `-e`, `-E` and `-v` are mainerr(ME_UNKNOWN_OPTION) like
# any other unknown letter, and `-e -s` with them, because `-s` was only ever read
# after `-e` had been.
#
# `-s` ALONE IS NOT DECLARED, and the plan's P3 row over-declares it: `case 's'`
# set silent mode only `if (exmode_active)` and called mainerr() otherwise, so a
# bare `-s` was an unknown option before this phase and is the same unknown option
# after it.  Measured: its record is byte-identical.  Nothing else moves -- not the
# other 26 command lines, not the 111 Ex-command rows, not the four pty scenarios,
# not the terminal table, and not one of the other 100 screen cases.
#
# TWO OF THE FOUR WOULD BE ABSORBED BY `stderr-moved` and are named anyway.  `-e`
# and `-e -s` exited 1 with an empty stream before this phase and exit 1 with an
# empty stream after it; only their stderr moved, which the line above already
# excuses everywhere.  They are ways INTO Ex mode and this is the phase that closes
# them, so the list says so rather than letting a dimension cover it -- and if a
# later phase ever drops `stderr-moved`, these two are already declared.
4     case:key_Q case:key_gQ
      argv:-e argv:-E argv:-e_-s argv:-v
#
# 5 leaves the command line as `+{command}` and `-T {term}`.  Three ways of naming
# something to edit go -- a file argument, a bare `-` and the `--` that made every
# word after it a file name -- and each becomes what every other unknown word
# already was, mainerr(ME_UNKNOWN_OPTION).  Six of the 30 command lines move and
# every one of them names a file or a stream:
#
#   `-` was the one row that BLOCKED: the editor read the keystroke file as buffer
#   text, closed fd 0 and waited on fd 2 for keys that never came (tools/zargv.py
#   says so).  It is now `Unknown option argument: "-"`, exit 1, and the row costs
#   the timeout no longer.
#   `f.txt` and `+q! f.txt` opened a buffer and drew a screen; both are exit 1 with
#   an empty stream now.
#   `--` and `-- +q!` ended the options, so `+q!` after `--` was a FILE NAME and not
#   a command -- which is why the two rows differ from each other in the baselines
#   and agree now.
#   `f.txt g.txt` is declared although `stderr-moved` would absorb it: it exited 1
#   with an empty stream before, for `Too many edit arguments: "g.txt"`, and exits 1
#   with an empty stream now for `Unknown option argument: "f.txt"`.  It is the
#   two-file-argument row and this is the phase that removes file arguments, so the
#   list says so rather than letting a dimension cover it.
#
# Nothing else moves, and the screen corpus is the reason it cannot: every case
# seeds itself by TYPING under `'paste'` (ZERO-PLAN.md 2d), so not one of the 102
# passes a file argument.  Measured: all 102 cases, all 111 Ex-command rows, the
# four pty scenarios and the terminal table are identical, as are the other 24
# command lines -- including every `+{command}` form and all three `-T` spellings.
5     argv:- argv:-- argv:f.txt argv:f.txt_g.txt argv:+q!_f.txt argv:--_+q!
#
# 6 removes every way to write a file: the six Ex commands `:write :wq :xit :exit
# :update :saveas`, and with them the nineteen functions the sweep finds unreachable
# once their rows are gone.  TWO SCREEN CASES AND SIX COMMAND ROWS MOVE, and the two
# kinds of movement are different:
#
#   `cmd_write` types `:write` WITH NO FILE NAME, so what the baselines hold is an
#   editor that FAILED to write, `E32: No file name`.  It is `E492: Not an editor
#   command: write` now.  That is the whole of what the corpus can see of this
#   phase, which is why pipes/zero6-check.sh runs both binaries in a directory it
#   keeps and requires the old one to leave a file on the disk.
#   `zz_key` moves because `nv_Zet` ran the command string `"x"`.  ZZ IS ZQ NOW, by
#   the user's decision, so the string is `"q!"`: one bell becomes none, E32 goes,
#   and the record loses two snapshots.  It moves whatever is done here -- left
#   alone, `ZZ` would answer E492 for a command that no longer exists -- so this
#   phase owns it.  ZERO-PLAN.md gives it to the `:q` phase; that row is annotated.
#   the six ref-excmds.txt rows do not change message: they CEASE TO EXIST.
#   tools/zexcmds.py enumerates the table through create_cmdidxs.names(), which
#   reads 105 names where it read 111, so there is no row to compare.
#
# Nothing else moves.  Measured with tools/zcompare.py: the other 100 screen cases,
# the other 105 command rows, all 30 command lines, the four pty scenarios and the
# terminal table are identical -- which includes `:q` on a modified buffer (E37,
# byte-identical: `check_changed` stays and is the `:q` phase's), `:read`, `:edit`,
# `:file` and CTRL-G.
6     case:cmd_write case:zz_key
      write wq xit exit update saveas
#
# 7 removes the way to read one: the Ex command `:read`, with the `:r !cmd` arm
# that was the last caller of the filter and shell plumbing whim left as stubs.
# TWO SCREEN CASES AND ONE COMMAND ROW MOVE:
#
#   `cmd_read` types `:read` WITH NO FILE NAME, so what the baselines hold is an
#   editor that FAILED to read, `E32: No file name` -- the shape phase 6 left
#   `:write` in.  It is `E492: Not an editor command: read` now.
#   `read_cmd_gone` types `:r !echo piped`, which whim's stub answered with
#   `E319: Sorry, the command is not available in this version`.  It is E492 now:
#   one snapshot fewer and the same single bell either side.
#   the ref-excmds.txt row `read` does not change message: it CEASES TO EXIST,
#   because tools/zexcmds.py enumerates the table and there are 104 names where
#   there were 105.
#
# `filter_gone` IS NOT DECLARED, and the plan's P6 row over-declares it: `:!` has
# not existed since whim, so `:%!sort` already answered E492 on the input binary
# and its record is byte-identical.  What this phase removes is the code behind a
# command that was already gone.
#
# Nothing else moves, and the corpus is the reason it cannot: every one of the 102
# cases types its own text and names no file, so not one of them can read one.
# Measured with tools/zcompare.py: the other 100 screen cases, the other 103
# command rows, all 30 command lines, the four pty scenarios and the terminal table
# are identical -- `:redo`, `:redraw`, `:registers` and `:reg` among them, which is
# the inheritance check a removed name asks for.
7     case:cmd_read case:read_cmd_gone
      read
#
# 8 removes every way to name something else to edit: the five Ex commands `:edit
# :enew :ex :visual :view` -- all one handler -- and the four Normal-mode keys that
# do the same thing from the buffer's own text, `gf gF [f ]f`.  TWO SCREEN CASES AND
# FIVE COMMAND ROWS MOVE, and the two kinds of movement are different:
#
#   `cmd_edit` types `:edit` WITH NO FILE NAME, so what the baselines hold is an
#   editor that got as far as check_changed() and refused, `E37: No write since last
#   change (add ! to override)`.  It is `E492: Not an editor command: edit` now.
#   `key_gf` presses `gf` on a word that names nothing, so what the baselines hold
#   is `E447: Can't find file "nosuchfile" in path` -- an editor that LOOKED.  The
#   key beeps from nv_g_cmd's `default: clearopbeep` now, where every other unused
#   `g` key does: the record loses one snapshot and keeps its ONE BELL either side,
#   so what moved is the screen and the snapshot count and not the bell.
#   the five ref-excmds.txt rows do not change message: they CEASE TO EXIST.
#   tools/zexcmds.py enumerates the table through create_cmdidxs' names(), which
#   reads 99 names where it read 104 -- and 99 is under the floor that tool had, so
#   this phase lowers it to 80 (ZERO-PLAN.md decision 8) in its own commit.
#
# Nothing else moves.  Measured with tools/zcompare.py: the other 100 screen cases,
# the other 94 command rows, all 30 command lines, the four pty scenarios and the
# terminal table are identical -- `:earlier`, `:vglobal`, `:vmap` and `:file` among
# them, which is the inheritance check five removed names ask for, and `:q` on a
# modified buffer (still E37: check_changed stays and is the `:q` phase's).
#
# THE CORPUS SEES TWO CASES AND NEITHER OPENS A FILE, which is why pipes/zero8-check.sh
# runs both binaries: `:e! keys`, `:ex! keys`, `:visual! keys` and `:view! keys` all
# load a file on the input binary, `:view` alone making `:set ro?` answer `readonly`,
# and `:enew!` empties the buffer -- that is the whole of do_exedit, measured from
# outside, and it is the only evidence that what went was opening a file rather than
# two error messages.
8     case:cmd_edit case:key_gf
      edit enew ex view visual
#
# 9 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and an empty declaration is a statement here rather
# than an omission.  This phase removes the file-reading path -- `readfile()`, 787
# lines, `read_buffer()`, the four functions of the message layer that reported what
# had been read, and eleven more the sweep finds under them -- and it declares
# nothing because THE PATH STOPPED BEING REACHABLE AT PHASE 8.  Phase 5 took the file
# argument and the bare `-`, phases 6, 7 and 8 took every command that could name a
# file, and after them `open_buffer()`'s two arms need either `b_ffname != NULL` or a
# `read_stdin` argument that all four callers pass as FALSE.  gcc kept the code
# because it cannot prove the first never holds; nothing the editor can be given
# reaches it.  So the difference this phase makes is between code that cannot run and
# code that is not there, and a recording that MOVED would mean the cut was wrong.
#
# WHICH MEANS THE DELTA CANNOT BE THE EVIDENCE, and pipes/zero9-check.sh says what is
# instead: the source this phase was handed, built twice with
# `(void)write(2, "READFILE-ENTERED\n", 17);` as the first statement of `readfile()`
# and then of `open_buffer()`, and recorded with tools/zrecord.sh.  Zero of the 106
# records carry the marker from `readfile()` and 104 of the same 106 carry it from
# `open_buffer()` through the identical instrument -- so the zero is a probe that can
# fail, and it is the whole of what this phase can prove.  Eight adversarial sessions
# that name the buffer after a real file run on both.
#
# Measured twice over: `diff -rq` of two full recordings, the binary the phase was
# handed against the one it made, is EMPTY -- all 102 screen cases, all 111 Ex-command
# rows, all 30 command lines, the four pty scenarios and the nineteen terminal rows --
# and tools/zerodelta.sh --phase 9 finds the same against whim-vim's frozen baselines.
#
# 10 takes the buffer's NAME: the one command that could still set it, `:file`, the
# three char_u* fields on every buffer, and the sixteen places that asked what the
# name was.  ONE SCREEN CASE AND ONE COMMAND ROW MOVE, and they are the two kinds:
#
#   `cmd_file` types `:file` with no argument, so what the baselines hold is the
#   CTRL-G line, `"[No Name]" [Modified] 1 line --100%--` -- an editor that answered
#   with the buffer's name.  It is `E492: Not an editor command: file` now: the same
#   exit, the same bells and the same snapshot count, with the stream 2,310 -> 2,327
#   bytes.
#   the ref-excmds.txt row `file` does not change message: it CEASES TO EXIST,
#   because tools/zexcmds.py enumerates the table and there are 98 names where there
#   were 99.
#
# NOTHING ELSE CAN MOVE, and the reason is stronger than a measurement: every fold
# this phase makes takes the branch the code already took at run time.  `b_ffname`,
# `b_sfname` and `b_fname` have been NULL since phase 5 took the file argument and
# `:file` was the last thing that could set one, so `== NULL` was already TRUE at
# every site and `!= NULL` already FALSE.  `[No Name]` survives as the ONLY answer
# buf_spname() can give rather than as one of two -- which is why CTRL-G, `g CTRL-G`,
# `:registers`, the `%` and `#` registers and the status line are byte-identical
# either side, and why `:registers` never printed its `"%` and `"#` lines to begin
# with.
#
# Measured with tools/zcompare.py: the other 101 screen cases, the other 97 command
# rows, all 30 command lines, the four pty scenarios and the terminal table are
# identical -- `:filter` and `:fixdel` among them, which is the inheritance check a
# removed name asks for, and `:q` on a modified buffer (still E37).
10    case:cmd_file
      file
#
# 11 removes the last refusal: `:q` on a modified buffer answered `E37: No write
# since last change (add ! to override)` and stayed.  ONE SCREEN CASE AND ONE
# COMMAND ROW MOVE, and the second is a THIRD kind of movement, which no zero phase
# has had before:
#
#   `quit_modified` types text and then `:q`, so what the baselines hold is an
#   editor that REFUSED.  The E37 line is gone, the one bell with it, and the record
#   loses a snapshot -- 2,342 -> 2,213 bytes of stream.  The exit status does NOT
#   move, and that is the corpus's limit rather than the phase's: every zcases.py
#   case ends with a trailing `:q!`, which quits the old binary too.  The status is
#   what pipes/zero11-check.sh's `q_alone` probe is for -- `:q` with NOTHING after
#   it, where the old binary draws E37, runs out of stdin, prints `Vim: Finished.`
#   and exits 1, and this one quits and exits 0.
#   the ref-excmds.txt row `quit` CHANGES MESSAGE AND DOES NOT CEASE TO EXIST,
#   unlike every row phases 6 to 10 declared: `:quit` is still a command and still
#   has its row, so tools/zexcmds.py enumerates the same 98 names and compares the
#   block.  Its `msgs` lose the E37 line -- `:set nopaste / E37... / :q!` becomes
#   `:set nopaste / :quit`.  The `cquit` row does not move.
#
# NOTHING ELSE CAN MOVE.  The fold takes the branch the code took whenever the
# buffer was unmodified, and `:q!` took it already; `ZZ` and `ZQ` have run
# `do_cmdline_cmd("q!")` since phase 6, so all four spellings were already one
# thing for an unmodified buffer and are one thing for every buffer now.  The
# buffer still KNOWS it is modified -- CTRL-G still prints `[Modified]`, the status
# line still draws `[+]`, `:set modified?` still answers -- so what went is the
# refusal and not the state.
#
# Measured with tools/zcompare.py: the other 101 screen cases, the other 97 command
# rows, all 30 command lines, the four pty scenarios and the terminal table are
# identical -- `:cquit` and `:quit`'s neighbours among them.
11    case:quit_modified
      quit
#
# 12 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and the reason is not phase 9's.  Phase 9 removed code
# that could not run; this phase removes code that CAN run and that the instrument
# cannot see.  Six option rows go -- `'fsync'`, `'prompt'`, `'readonly'`,
# `'undoreload'`, `'write'` and `'writeany'`, the six of a computed seven whose global
# nothing reads -- and with `'readonly'` go the `W10: Warning: Changing a readonly
# file` message, the second it paused for, and the `[RO]`/`[readonly]` indicators on
# the CTRL-G line and the status line.
#
# WHY NONE OF THAT MOVES A RECORD, measured record by record:
#
#   `:set <name>?` goes from an answer to `E518: Unknown option`, and NO RECORDED
#   CASE OR ROW ASKS ANY OF THE SIX.
#   BARE `:set` DOES NOT MOVE, because none of the six differs from its default, and
#   its listing is wiped by the Press-ENTER redraw before tools/zscreen.py takes its
#   picture anyway.
#   `:set all` DOES move -- `readonly`, `fsync`, `prompt` and `undoreload` are in the
#   old stream and absent from the new -- and `:set all` is in no harness.  It is a
#   probe.
#   THE W10 WARNING AND THE TWO INDICATORS MOVE, and no recorded case sets
#   `'readonly'`: they need `:set ro`, which nothing types.
#   tools/zexcmds.py records `exit`, `bells`, `stderr`, `text` and `msgs` for the
#   `set` row and NO STREAM DIGEST, so even a change to what `:set` prints in the
#   stream would be invisible there.
#
# So a phase that did nothing and a phase that did everything have the same
# recording, and pipes/zero12-check.sh says what the evidence is instead: 26 probes on
# both binaries, of which `ro_w10` is the one that shows BEHAVIOUR going -- `:set ro`
# on an unmodified buffer and then an insert draws W10 and pauses 1,008 ms on the
# binary this phase was handed and 3 ms here, the same shape as phase 2's 2,010 -> 5.
#
# THE FLAG LETTERS ARE NOT TOUCHED AND THAT IS A DECISION, not an omission.  A letter
# in `'cpoptions'`' or `'shortmess'`'s validity list with no enumerator is accepted
# and means nothing -- 23 of 60 and 14 of 23 here, and this phase makes exactly one
# more so, `'shortmess'`'s `r`.  Removing a letter from a list could not move `:set
# cpo?` or `:set shm?`, the value being a different literal, but it WOULD turn `:set
# shm=F` from silently accepted into E539, and nothing in the instrument types `:set
# shm=`.  Accepting a letter that does nothing is what upstream does for every feature
# a build lacks.
#
# Measured: `diff -rq` over two full recordings, the binary the phase was handed
# against the one it made, is EMPTY -- all 102 screen cases, all 111 Ex-command rows,
# all 30 command lines, the four pty scenarios and the nineteen terminal rows -- and
# tools/zerodelta.sh --phase 12 finds the same against whim-vim's frozen baselines,
# with the nine lines phases 2 to 11 declared and nothing new.
#
# 13 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, for phase 9's reason and not phase 12's.  Two `static
# FILE *` survive in this editor and NOTHING HAS EVER OPENED EITHER OF THEM IN ANY
# BUILD OF zero-vim: `scriptin[NSCRIPT]`, which `-s {scriptfile}` filled and for which
# whim removed the option, and `redir_fd`, which `:redir > file` filled and for which
# whim removed the command.  `scriptin[]` is assigned in exactly one place in the
# whole file and that place sets it to NULL; `redir_fd`'s only assignment is its own
# declaration.  So removing them is the difference between code that cannot run and
# code that is not there, and a recording that MOVED would mean the cut was wrong.
#
# WHICH MEANS THE DELTA CANNOT BE THE EVIDENCE, and pipes/zero13-check.sh says what is
# instead: the source this phase was handed, built twice with
# `(void)write(2, "FILESTAR-ENTERED\n", 17);` at FIVE places -- the top of
# `closescript()`, inside `inchar()`'s `getc(scriptin[curscript])` loop, inside
# `redir_write()`'s `redirecting()` block, inside `undo_cmdmod`'s, and the top of
# `vim_fsync()` -- and then with the identical instrument on `ui_write()`, which every
# byte the editor draws goes through.  Zero of the 106 records carry the marker from
# the five and 105 of the same 106 carry it from `ui_write()`, so the zero is a probe
# that can fail.  Eighteen adversarial sessions -- every one of them a way of making
# the editor PRINT, which is where `redir_write()` sat -- run on both.
#
# Measured twice over: `diff -rq` of two full recordings, the binary the phase was
# handed against the one it made, is EMPTY -- all 102 screen cases, all 111 Ex-command
# rows, all 30 command lines, the four pty scenarios and the nineteen terminal rows --
# and tools/zerodelta.sh --phase 13 finds the same against whim-vim's frozen baselines.
#
# Four libc symbols go with it, `fclose getc putc fsync`, and after them the core has
# no `open`, no `stat`, no stdio stream and no fourth descriptor: it can read, write,
# close and dup fds 0, 1 and 2 and nothing else.  `fputs` does NOT go and the check
# asserts it STILL undefined: the source names it nowhere and gcc lowers
# `fprintf(stderr, "...")` to it.
#
# 14 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and the reason is a THIRD one -- neither phase 9's nor
# phase 12's.  Phase 9 removed code that could not run.  Phase 12 removed code that
# can run and that the instrument cannot see.  This phase REMOVES NO CODE AND CHANGES
# NO BEHAVIOUR: it brings sixteen libc string and memory functions into the file as
# `static musl_*` written from /root/musl/src/string/, and moves `sprintf` onto the
# printf this editor already carries.  A recording that MOVED would mean a vendored
# function was wrong.
#
# Measured twice over: `diff -rq` of two full recordings, the binary the phase was
# handed against the one it made, is EMPTY -- all 102 screen cases, all 111
# Ex-command rows, all 30 command lines, the four pty scenarios and the nineteen
# terminal rows -- and tools/zerodelta.sh --phase 14 finds the same against
# whim-vim's frozen baselines.  Not one enumerator moves either, because nothing is
# deleted: no type, no enum, no cmdnames[] row, no options[] row.
#
# SEVENTEEN LIBC SYMBOLS GO, `memchr memcmp memcpy memmove memset sprintf strcasecmp
# strcat strchr strcmp strcpy strlen strncasecmp strncmp strncpy strpbrk strstr`, and
# `nm -u` goes 61 -> 44.  That it is SEVENTEEN and not fifteen is a measurement and
# was the open question: gcc emits `memcpy` and `memset` for ITSELF, for aggregate
# assignments and large zero initialisers, whatever the source calls -- and in this
# file it emits none, so nothing had to be defined under a real name and
# "nothing is global but main()" is untouched.  pipes/zero14-check.sh asserts it from
# the assembly as well as from nm.
#
# ONE THING DOES CHANGE ON ONE REACHABLE INPUT, AND IT IS A BUG FIX, so it is written
# here rather than left to the commit message.  `t_CF` is a user-settable option that
# `term_font()` uses as a FORMAT STRING into `char buf[20]`; `sprintf` has no bound.
# `:set t_CF=` + forty X + `%d`, then `:highlight Search ctermfont=3` and a search,
# exits -11 (SIGSEGV) on the binary this phase was handed and exits 0 here with the
# output truncated to nineteen characters.  It is NOT a declared delta because
# nothing in the instrument sets `t_CF` and the only built-in `t_CF` is the `debug`
# terminal's "[CF%d]"; it is a MUST-DIFFER probe in the check, with both halves
# required.  The same probe records that a user-set `%f`, `%b`, `%*d` or `%z` now
# renders as vim's own printf spells it rather than as musl's, while `%d` and `%1$d`
# are identical -- and that `%s` segfaults on BOTH binaries and is not this phase's.

# 15 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is a THIRD KIND of empty declaration.  Phase 9's
# was code that COULD NOT RUN -- readfile() was already unreachable.  Phase 13's was a
# POSSIBILITY THAT HAD NEVER EXISTED -- neither `FILE *` had been opened in any build.
# This one is an EQUALITY.  The code it replaces runs constantly: `towupper` alone is
# entered 892 times in a trivial session, from `utf_islower()` inside
# `buf_init_chartab()`, BEFORE 'casemap' has been applied and with arguments in exactly
# 128..255.  What is claimed is that eleven libc functions and six <ctype.h> macros
# have been replaced by C that computes the same answer.
#
# SO THE EVIDENCE IS EQUIVALENCE, NOT UNREACHABILITY, and pipes/zero15-check.sh says
# what it is.  tools/muslcase.py re-derives all 1,114,112 codepoints from THIS
# MACHINE'S libc through ctypes and compares them with the 187 + 171 convertStruct rows
# the phase shipped; tools/muslctype.py slices the seventeen functions OUT OF THE
# PRODUCED SOURCE, compiles them with -Wall -Wextra and runs them beside libc's over
# 1,002,140 int values, 137,560 atoi/atol strings and 1,845 bsearch lookups compared by
# POINTER.  Both are proven able to fail.
#
# AND THE CORPUS CANNOT SEE ANY OF IT, which is why fourteen probe sessions run on both
# binaries beside the recording.  Every one of the 102 screen cases seeds itself by
# TYPING ASCII, so nothing in it reaches Unicode case folding, 'casemap', the four
# bsearch tables or the sort.  The probes do: six over an ASCII, a Latin-1, a Greek, a
# Cyrillic, a circled Latin and a Coptic letter under every 'casemap' the option can
# hold -- the first three separate an ASCII fallback and the last two separate vim's
# own table, and a text with only one group would pass one of the two wrong answers --
# four for the four bsearch tables, three for atoi and atol, one for the chartab those
# 892 startup calls build, and :undolist read out of the RAW STREAM, because the
# Press-ENTER redraw wipes it before the \x1b[?25h that ends a step.
#
# Measured: two full recordings, the binary the phase was handed against the one it
# made, are byte-identical -- all 102 screen cases, all 111 Ex-command rows, all 30
# command lines, the four pty scenarios and the nineteen terminal rows -- and
# tools/zerodelta.sh --phase 15 finds the same against whim-vim's frozen baselines.
#
# Eleven libc symbols go, `atoi atol bsearch isalnum iscntrl ispunct qsort tolower
# toupper towlower towupper`, and FIVE MORE identifiers leave the source with no symbol
# to show for it: musl spells isalpha, isdigit, isgraph, islower and isupper as macros.
# That is why <ctype.h> could not be removed by counting nm -u, and why this phase's
# check asserts the header contract itself.
#
# 16 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is a FOURTH kind of empty declaration.  Three
# phases before it declared nothing, each for a different reason, and the reason is
# the statement: 9 removed code that COULD NOT RUN, every way to name a file having
# gone at phase 8; 12 removed code that CAN run and that this instrument CANNOT SEE,
# nothing recorded ever asking `:set ro?`; 13 removed the POSSIBILITY, two `FILE *`
# that had never been opened in any build of zero-vim.
#
# THIS PHASE CHANGES NO CODE AT ALL.  It removes six `#include` lines and one typedef
# of a type nothing uses, and its evidence is not that the recording did not move but
# that THE BINARY IS THE SAME BYTES -- the input's and the output's, both built with
# SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=0 and the boundary's own flags, compared with cmp.  That is tier 1
# of CLAUDE.md's verification table, and it subsumes every screen case, every
# Ex-command row, every command line and every pty scenario at once, because the
# program that would be run is literally the same program.  tools/zerodelta.sh --phase
# 16 runs and finds nothing moved, as it must; here it corroborates rather than proves.
#
# Phases 14 and 15 declare nothing for a fifth reason again, and it is the weakest of
# the five: their code changes and their binary moves, and the claim is that
# twenty-eight replacement implementations do what the ones they replace did.  Their
# own checks are where that is argued.  This phase's claim is the only one in the
# pipeline that a byte comparison can settle outright.
#
# 17 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is phase 13's kind rather than phase 9's.  Phase 9
# removed code that had BECOME unreachable -- `readfile()` was live at the start of this
# pipeline and phases 5 to 8 took every way to reach it.  This phase, like 13, removes a
# POSSIBILITY THAT HAS NEVER EXISTED IN ANY BUILD OF zero-vim: `deathtrap()`'s
# `entered >= 3` ladder, which is `reset_signals()`, `_exit(8)` and `exit(7)`.
#
# TWO FACTS MAKE IT UNREACHABLE AND NEITHER IS ZERO'S DOING.  `catch_signals()` installs
# the deadly handler with `sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask)` and `sa.sa_flags = 0` -- no
# `SA_NODEFER` -- so a deadly signal is blocked for the duration of its own handler; and
# `signal_info[]` carries exactly TWO `deadly = TRUE` rows, SIGHUP and SIGTERM, because
# whim removed SIGSEGV, SIGBUS, SIGILL and SIGFPE and all four are at zero mentions.  Two
# signals, each blocked in its own handler, lets `entered` reach 2 -- the
# `Vim: Double signal, exiting` arm, which calls getout(1) and never returns -- and no
# further.
#
# WHERE PHASE 13'S ARGUMENT WAS TEXTUAL, THIS ONE IS ABOUT SIGNAL MASKS, so it is
# measured rather than read.  pipes/zero17-check.sh instruments the source this phase was
# HANDED with `write(2, "DTn\n", 4)` after `++entered;` and `write(2, "DTLADDER\n", 9)`
# inside the ladder, and builds it five ways.  Bombarded -- eight concurrent sessions,
# sixty alternating SIGTERM/SIGHUP each at full speed -- the maximum `entered` ever
# observed is 2 and DTLADDER never appears.  Forced -- a `raise()` inside
# `preserve_exit()` and another in the `entered == 2` arm -- it stops at 2 and exits 1.
# THE SAME SOURCE WITH ONE FIELD CHANGED, `sa.sa_flags = SA_NODEFER`, reaches depth 3,
# enters the ladder and EXITS 7, and one forced signal further EXITS 8: both statements
# this phase deletes are live code that only the signal mask keeps out of reach.  That
# pair is the probe proving it can fail.
#
# AND THE DEADLY SIGNALS THEMSELVES DO NOT MOVE.  A single SIGTERM and a single SIGHUP
# give the binary this phase was handed and the one it made the same exit status and the
# same stdout and stderr byte for byte, with `Vim: Caught deadly signal TERM`/`HUP` and
# `Vim: Finished.` required to be in what was drawn -- so the equality is not two empty
# streams agreeing.  tools/zerodelta.sh --phase 17 finds the corpus unmoved, as it must.
#
# `_exit` goes with the nine lines and `exit` does NOT: `mch_exit`'s `exit(r);` is the
# one call left in the file, and demoting it is a later phase's.  The word `exit` is
# useless as a source assertion -- two string literals, a `goto exit;` and its `exit:`
# label in vim_regsub_both() carry four of its five remaining mentions -- so what this
# phase asserts is `nm -u`.
#
# 18 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is a SIXTH kind of empty declaration -- the one
# where there is nothing to declare because the phase moves no code and removes none.
# `main()` becomes `static int vim_main(int argc, char **argv)` with its body unchanged
# byte for byte, and a six-line `main()` whose whole content is
# `return vim_main(argc, argv);` is appended below it.  The editor runs one call frame
# deeper and does exactly what it did.
#
# The five before it each removed SOMETHING and the reason each declared nothing was
# different: 9 removed code that COULD NOT RUN, 12 removed code that CAN run and that
# this instrument CANNOT SEE, 13 removed a POSSIBILITY, 16 changed no code at all and
# its binary is the same bytes, and 14 and 15 replaced code with code that computes the
# same answers.  THIS ONE ADDS A FRAME AND THAT IS ALL, so its binary is NOT the same
# bytes -- at -O0 a call is real code -- and 16's tier-1 argument is not available.
#
# What is available instead is every way the editor can end, probed on both binaries:
# `:q!` 0, `:cq 3` 3, EOF 1, a bad option 1, SIGTERM 1, SIGHUP 1 -- ex_quit, ex_cquit,
# read_error_exit, mainerr and deathtrap twice, which is every termination route
# ZERO-PLAN.md maps that a probe can reach from outside.  `return vim_main(argc, argv);`
# puts a value in the program's path that was not there before, and that table is what
# says it arrives.  The table is PROVEN able to fail: the output built a second time
# with mch_exit's `exit(r)` changed to `exit(r + 1)` moves all six.
#
# 19 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is 18's kind: the phase moves no code out and
# changes no message.  `mch_exit()`'s last statement stops being `exit(r);` and becomes
# `vim_host_exit(r);`, a call through a function pointer the launcher installs; the
# launcher lands on `__builtin_setjmp` and RETURNS the status instead of the process
# ending inside the editor.  Everything mch_exit does before that line -- the terminal
# restored, the screen scrolled, the memfile closed -- is unchanged, so what the editor
# DRAWS on its way out cannot move, and the recording is of what the editor draws.
#
# What CAN move is the exit status, and that is measured rather than declared: six
# routes on both binaries, `:q!` 0, `:cq 3` 3, EOF 1, a bad option 1, SIGTERM 1,
# SIGHUP 1, with the output built again with `host_code = r;` made `host_code = r + 1;`
# required to move all six.  A status is not a dimension of this instrument -- zcases
# and zexcmds record it per case and zerodelta compares it -- so an unchanged status is
# part of "nothing moved" and the probe table is what makes it legible.
#
# 18 and 19 are the first two phases in this pipeline whose empty declaration means
# NEITHER "nothing ran" NOR "the instrument cannot see it": the code runs, the
# instrument sees it, and it does the same thing.

# 20 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is the same kind as 18 and 19: the code runs, the
# instrument sees it run, and it does the same thing.  Every keystroke now reaches the
# core through `musl_read_input()`, every wait is `musl_wait_for_input()`, the terminal
# is put in raw mode by `musl_term_start()` and given back by `musl_term_stop()`, and
# the editor installs no signal handler of its own -- and two full recordings either
# side are byte-identical, 102 screen cases, ref-excmds.txt, ref-argv.txt, ref-pty.txt
# and ref-term.txt.
#
# BUT IT IS ALSO PHASE 2'S KIND, and that is the part that matters.  Most of what this
# phase changes the instrument CANNOT see: on a pipe `tcgetattr`/`tcsetattr` fail and
# change nothing, no recorded case sends a signal, no recorded case resizes a window,
# no recorded case types `gs`, and no recorded case reaches end of input with a
# terminal on fd 2.  So the phase owes probes, and pipes/zero20-check.sh runs fifteen
# of them on both binaries: seven that MUST differ and eight that MUST NOT.
#
# ONE BEHAVIOUR REALLY GOES AND IT IS PROBE-ONLY.  `fill_input_buf`'s `close(0);
# vim_ignored = dup(2);` arm reopened the editor's stdin from its stderr when stdin hit
# end of file and was not a terminal.  With a TERMINAL on fd 2 the binary this phase
# was handed reopens fd 0 and carries on editing -- 2,016 bytes of drawn screen -- and
# this one prints `Vim: Finished.` and exits 1, 168 bytes.  It fired 0 times across all
# 253 recorded rows, which is why the declaration is empty and the probe is the
# evidence.  What it buys is ZERO-PLAN.md 4b in its strongest form: with `close` and
# `dup` gone the core cannot open, close or duplicate any descriptor at all.
#
# THE OTHER SIX MUST-DIFFER PROBES ARE THE PHASE WORKING.  `:set trz?` and `:set
# trz=sigwinch` become E518, 'termresize' being the one options[] row this phase takes.
# `ESC [ 48;30;100 t` was buffer text and now resizes the editor, because the mode-2048
# notification arm is unconditional -- the negotiation that guarded it could never run.
# `ESC [ ? 1 z` was nothing and now runs `:stop`, which is the private sequence the
# host sends when it catches SIGTSTP.  CTRL-C with stdout a pipe ran `:qa` (E492) and
# now draws the message, `stdout_isatty` having folded to TRUE.  And an external
# `kill -TSTP` draws a different number of bytes, which is what says it took the
# in-band path rather than the core's own `got_tstp` flag.


# 21 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is 18, 19 and 20's kind: the code runs, the
# instrument sees it run, and it does the same thing.  Every byte this editor has ever
# put on a stream instead of a screen -- 20 statements in five functions -- now goes
# through one `vim_host_message(msg, len, err)` the launcher installs and
# `host_message()` performs with `write(err ? 2 : 1, ...)`, and two full recordings
# either side are BYTE-IDENTICAL: `diff -r` reports 0 lines across 102 screen cases,
# ref-excmds.txt, ref-argv.txt, ref-pty.txt and ref-term.txt.  Only the syscall
# underneath the bytes changes.
#
# AND THE INSTRUMENT CAN SEE IT, which is what separates this from phases 2, 12 and 20.
# 24 of the 30 ref-argv.txt rows really do carry a message -- 23 mainerr and one
# report_term_error -- and the phase proves it marks the SAME 24 either side, with the
# input built with `write(2, "MESSAGE-OUT\n", 12)` at all nineteen output statements and
# the output with the identical instrument inside host_message().  So an empty
# declaration here is not blindness; it is the strongest kind this pipeline has short of
# phase 16's byte-identical binary.
#
# THE PHASE OWES A CONTROL FOR EXACTLY THAT REASON, and it is one character:
# `write(err ? 2 : 1, ...)` made `write(err ? 1 : 1, ...)` moves all 24 records, 217
# lines of `diff -r` -- where tools/zerodelta.sh names only 14 of them, ten of the 24
# being argv rows phases 4 and 5 already declared and tools/zcompare.py therefore no
# longer comparing.  That gap is why pipes/zero21-check.sh diffs the two recordings
# itself and keeps zerodelta.sh as the second opinion.
#
# ONE THING REALLY CHANGES AND NO INSTRUMENT IN THIS PIPELINE CAN SEE IT: mainerr and
# report_term_error assemble into a 1024-byte buffer, so a message built from an argv
# string longer than about 930 characters is now capped.  MEASURED on both binaries: an
# unknown option of 900 characters is byte-identical and one of 2,000 is 2,095 bytes on
# the input and 1,023 here; a `-T` of 2,000 is 2,039 against 1,023.  It is not declared,
# because nothing in the corpus is within 800 characters of it and `pipes/zero.delta` is
# a list of records that moved; it is pinned as a probe in both directions instead.

# 22 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is phase 2's kind rather than phase 9's: the code
# runs, and the instrument can barely see it.  Seven wrapper functions that took `...`,
# opened a `va_list` and handed it to `vim_vsnprintf` are expanded at their 129 call
# sites into `vim_snprintf(...)` plus the tail the wrapper ran afterwards -- `msg()`,
# `msg_attr()`, `msg_attr_keep()`, `emsg()`, `iemsg()`, all five of which already
# existed and were already called from elsewhere, so no message logic is written here at
# all.  `va_start` goes from eight functions to one.  Two full recordings either side
# are byte-identical: 102 screen cases, ref-excmds.txt, ref-argv.txt, ref-pty.txt and
# ref-term.txt.
#
# HOW BLIND THE RECORDING IS, MEASURED rather than asserted.  The input source built
# again with `write(2, "ZW|<wrapper>|<format>\n", ...)` at the entry to each of the
# seven, run over the 102 screen cases: `vim_snprintf_safelen` is entered 617 times,
# `smsg_attr_keep` 6, `vim_snprintf_add` 2, and `smsg`, `smsg_attr`, `semsg` and
# `siemsg` NOT ONCE.  `semsg` is 94 of the 129 sites and the screen corpus never enters
# it.  So the phase owes probes, and pipes/zero22-check.sh runs 263 of them on both
# binaries -- the Ex-command errors, 132 regexp errors over both engines and three magic
# settings (which are where the 18 comma-expression sites live), the report messages,
# the substitute-confirm prompt, undo, CTRL-G and the ruler, and four incsearch probes
# with a bad pattern, which are the only way to reach a `semsg` under `emsg_off > 0`.
# The same instrument says the probes enter `semsg` 232 times over 34 distinct formats
# and reach 67 of the 129 sites -- 66 found by mapping each runtime format string back
# through the `static char e_*[]` table to the site's format expression, plus
# `vim_snprintf_add`'s single site, whose format is a parameter and which the instrument
# shows entered twice.  The other 62 are covered by the edit being ONE RULE applied
# uniformly, and by the whole-file equalities the check makes: `semsg`'s unreached sites
# are out-of-memory reports, the twenty inside the formatter itself -- which fire only
# on a format string the editor would have to have got wrong, and every format in this
# file is one of its own -- and `siemsg`'s ten internal errors, which is the memfile
# detecting its own corruption.
#
# THE PROBES ARE PROVEN ABLE TO FAIL, WITH FOUR CONTROLS, AND TWO OF THEM MOVE NOTHING
# ON PURPOSE.  This phase's own output with both room helpers returning 20 instead of
# IOSIZE moves 129 of 263; with every `semsg` site given `msg()` for a tail instead of
# `emsg()` it moves 155.  With `safelen_result`'s clamp removed it moves 0, and with all
# three guards removed it moves 0 -- and those two are reported rather than dropped,
# because they are the honest statement of what the evidence cannot reach: the clamp
# needs a message longer than 1,025 bytes out of `fileinfo`, and the guards need
# `IObuff == NULL`, which is an out-of-memory failure of the first two allocations the
# process makes.
#
# NOTHING MOVES IN `nm -u` EITHER, and that is the other half of the same fact.  A pure
# restructure inside one translation unit frees nothing: `vim_vsnprintf_typval` still
# does every conversion in the same file, and `<stdarg.h>`'s three names are macros and
# a compiler builtin type, which are no symbol at all.  What the phase moves is not code
# across a boundary but the POSSIBILITY of drawing one.
#
# 23 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is the strongest kind of nothing this pipeline has:
# THE BINARY IS BYTE-IDENTICAL.  `NULL` becomes `nullptr`, a C23 keyword that needs no
# declaration; `size_t` becomes `usize`, with one new line, `typedef typeof(sizeof(0))
# usize;`; and the thirty `(void *)NULL` become plain `nullptr`, the cast having existed
# for the variadic hazard of an UNTYPED null constant and `nullptr` being typed.  Three
# thousand mechanical edits, +2 lines, and `cmp` of the input's binary against the
# output's -- both built with SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=0 and the boundary's own flags -- finds
# no difference at all: 788,488 bytes either side.
#
# SO THE DELTA CANNOT BE THE EVIDENCE AND IS NOT ASKED TO BE.  Tier 1 of CLAUDE.md's
# verification table subsumes every screen case, every Ex-command row, every command
# line and every pty scenario at once, because the program that would be run is the same
# program; tools/zerodelta.sh --phase 23 corroborates.
#
# WHAT COULD HAVE MOVED IS DATA, AND THAT IS THE ONE THING THIS PHASE HAD TO GET RIGHT.
# Three string literals in this file contain `NULL` -- the E1507 internal-error message,
# `"[NULL]"` and `"NULL"` -- and a line-wise sed rewrites all three.  Measured: the
# binary then differs by 1,598 bytes, 1,354 of them in `.rodata`, and `strings` finds
# `[nullptr]` and an E1507 message that names a C keyword at the user.  With the three
# excluded the binary is `cmp`-identical.  pipes/zero23-check.sh builds the
# literal-unaware form as a control and requires it to differ, which is what keeps the
# equality above from being two numbers agreeing.
#
# 24 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is phase 23's kind: THE BINARY IS BYTE-IDENTICAL,
# 788,488 bytes either side, built with SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=0 and the boundary's own flags.
# 113 `__attribute__((unused))` are deleted, 20 `__attribute__((fallthrough));` are
# respelled as the C23 `[[fallthrough]];`, and 6 `format`/`format_arg` are kept -- 139
# attributes to 6, 117 lines changed, not one line added or removed, and not one
# statement changed.  An attribute of any of these three kinds emits no code: `unused`
# suppresses a diagnostic, `fallthrough` gives one a hint, and `format` decides what gcc
# will check.  So tier 1 of CLAUDE.md's verification table applies in full and subsumes
# every screen case, every Ex-command row, every command line and every pty scenario at
# once; tools/zerodelta.sh --phase 24 corroborates.
#
# AND THE BINARY IS BLIND TO THE ONE DECISION HERE THAT COULD BE WRONG, which is why the
# phase does not rest on it alone.  MEASURED: removing the six survivors as well leaves
# the binary STILL cmp-identical, while `-Wformat=2` goes from 115 `-Wformat-nonliteral`
# warnings to zero.  The evidence for keeping them is therefore the warnings and not the
# bytes: the identical 115 in the identical 53 functions before and after -- phase 22's
# invariant -- with two controls that break it in opposite directions, vim_snprintf's
# `format(printf, 3, 4)` removed giving 0 and `_()`'s `format_arg(1)` removed giving 135.
#
# 25 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is NOT phase 23's and 24's kind: THE BINARY IS NOT
# BYTE-IDENTICAL AND THE PHASE DOES NOT AIM FOR IT.  The core reached the host through
# two function pointers -- `vim_host_exit`, phase 19's, and `vim_host_message`, phase
# 21's -- each a file-scope object installed through a parameter of `vim_main()` that
# `main()` passed.  The one reason they were pointers is gone: pipes/zero19-edit.sh put
# it plainly, "a pointer the launcher installs through a parameter adds no external
# symbol, where a `musl_exit(int)` the host defines would", which was true of a design
# with TWO TRANSLATION UNITS.  ZERO-PLAN.md 4c is now ONE FILE with the first `#include`
# as the boundary, so the host's definitions sit below the core in the same translation
# unit and a `static` forward declaration plus a direct call does the same job with less
# machinery and no global to avoid.  Two objects, two parameters, two assignments and two
# arguments go; two prototypes arrive, at the end of the nine `musl_` ones phase 20 left,
# so the core -> host boundary is ONE block of eleven declarations.  80,178 -> 80,173
# lines, and `vim_main(int argc, char **argv)` is the signature phase 18 wrote.
#
# THE BINARY IS THE SAME SIZE AND NOT THE SAME BYTES, both measured: 788,488 either side,
# 347,279 of them differing.  An indirect call through a pointer loads the pointer and
# calls a register where a direct call is a relative call to a known address, and
# removing two file-scope objects moves what follows them; at -O0 that is simply
# different code.  So this phase cannot use tier 1 of CLAUDE.md's verification table, and
# its evidence is what every zero phase before 23 used -- TWO FULL RECORDINGS, `diff -r`
# empty across all 106 records: the 102 screen cases, every Ex command typed at `:`,
# every command line the parser may see, the four pty scenarios and the terminal table.
#
# AND IT CAN FAIL, ONCE FOR EACH NAME THE PHASE MAKES DIRECT, which is what keeps an
# empty `diff -r` from being a harness that recorded nothing.  host_exit's own statement
# `host_code = r;` changed to `r + 1` moves 105 of the 106 records -- every one but the
# terminal table, which records no exit status -- and host_message's `write(err ? 2 : 1,
# ...)` with the two streams swapped moves ref-argv.txt AND NOTHING ELSE, which is phase
# 21's finding read back: everything reaching that function is a message printed before
# there is a screen.
#
# THE PHASE FREES NO SYMBOL AND SAYS SO AS AN EQUALITY.  `nm -u` is the same set as a cmp
# in both directions, and `exit` does not come back -- the host still records a status
# and jumps, phase 19's __builtin_longjmp launcher being untouched here.  `nm
# --extern-only --defined-only` is still exactly `main`, which is the assertion that
# matters most in a phase that makes the core NAME the host's two functions: with
# `static` off the two prototypes gcc refuses, and with it off the prototypes and the
# definitions the build is silent and two symbols become external.  pipes/zero25-check.sh
# builds both.
#
# 26 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is a SIXTH kind: not code that could not run (9,
# 17), not code the instrument cannot see (12), not a possibility removed (13), not a
# `cmp` of the binary (16, 23, 24), and not phase 25's "the code runs, the instrument
# sees it, and it does the same thing" either -- it is SIX OF SEVEN CHANGES THAT ARE A
# `cmp` AND ONE THAT IS A RECORDING, and the phase separates them rather than taking the
# weaker evidence for all of it.  The core stops taking eight things from a header:
# `time_t` becomes `typedef long time_T`, `sig_atomic_t` is spelled `volatile int`,
# `uintptr_t` becomes `usize`, `MIN` and `MAX` are expanded at 19 lines to the text the
# preprocessor gives for <sys/param.h>, `offsetof` becomes `__builtin_offsetof`, and the
# nine libc functions the core still calls get plain prototypes.  Every one of those is a
# rename or an expansion the preprocessor was already performing, so none of them can
# generate a different instruction -- and the check states that as an equality rather
# than as a claim: with the SEVENTH change alone reverted the binary is `cmp`-IDENTICAL
# to the 788,488-byte one the phase was handed.
#
# THE SEVENTH IS THE CLOCK, and it is the only libc type the core could not simply rename
# away.  `struct timeval` is a LAYOUT, so `elapsed_T` becomes the core's own TAGLESS
# `struct { long tv_sec; long tv_usec; }` and the five `gettimeofday(&X, nullptr)` calls
# go through a new `musl_gettimeofday(long *, long *)` in the host block.  That is a call
# and two stores where there was a syscall wrapper, so the binary moves -- 788,488 either
# side -- and the evidence for it is TWO FULL RECORDINGS, `diff -r` empty across all 106
# records.
#
# AND THE RECORDING IS NOT BLIND TO IT, which is what keeps that empty `diff -r` from
# being two numbers agreeing.  `musl_gettimeofday` writing its two fields the wrong way
# round moves SIX of the 102 screen cases -- key_Q, key_gQ, key_gf, macro_q, reg_percent
# and ruler_move -- and stalls tools/zpty.py outright, an editor whose timeouts never
# expire being exactly what a backwards clock makes.  The control is zcases.py alone for
# that reason: the pty harness is right to hang on it, and a check should not depend on
# how long it takes to give up.
#
# THE TWELVE DERIVED CONSTANTS ARE NOT IN THIS PHASE and cannot be: `enum : int { INT_MAX
# = ... };` placed after `#include <limits.h>` is `enum : int { 0x7fffffff = ... };`, a
# syntax error.  They are phase 27's, with the move, and they are the only
# header-supplied names this phase leaves above the host block.
#
# 27 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is the SEVENTH kind -- one no earlier zero phase
# has had, because none of them moved code without changing a line of it.  Phase 16's
# and 23's empty declarations were a byte-identical BINARY, which is CLAUDE.md's tier 1
# and subsumes every case at once; this phase cannot have that, because it moves 1,800
# lines of definitions past the rest of the file and every address below the first of
# them moves.  What it has instead is tier 1 ONE LEVEL UP, on the SOURCE: the output is
# the input's lines, rearranged.  Stated as a multiset and checked as one -- not one of
# the input's 80,197 lines is missing from the output, and the only lines the output
# adds are the THIRTY-TWO this phase writes, twenty enumerator lines and twelve
# static_asserts, plus three blanks where an emptied paragraph left two.  A phase that
# moved code and altered a character of it on the way could not say that, and the
# recording is what answers for the thirty-two.
#
# WHAT MOVES.  The eleven `#include`s go down to just above the host block, and THE
# FIRST OF THEM IS THE BOUNDARY between the core and the host -- nothing else marks it
# (ZERO-PLAN.md 4c).  With them go the four functions that hold a `va_list`, which the
# core cannot declare: vim_snprintf, vim_vsnprintf, vim_vsnprintf_typval and
# skip_to_arg.  And with THOSE goes whatever only they use, computed to a fixpoint by
# compiling the cut and moving what gcc calls unused -- five rounds, 15 functions, 18
# objects and 3 enum blocks, the formatter's whole private island.  Up go the twelve
# constants the core used to take from <limits.h>, <stdint.h>, <stdlib.h>, <signal.h>
# and <limits.h>'s PATH_MAX: eight derived from the type system and four asserted, all
# twelve enumerators because PATH_MAX is an ARRAY BOUND.
#
# AND THE RECORDING IS THE ONLY THING THAT CAN ANSWER FOR IT, which is why this phase
# is 25's and 21's kind and not 16's: the code runs, the instrument sees it, and it does
# the same thing.  Two full recordings, of the binary the phase was handed and of its
# own, are byte-identical across all 106 records -- and the binary is 788,488 bytes on
# both sides, the same size and not the same bytes.  `nm -u` is the same 17 names in
# both directions, because moving a definition inside ONE translation unit frees nothing
# and needs nothing: THE CLAIM OF THIS PHASE IS STRUCTURAL AND NOT A SYMBOL COUNT.
#
# WHAT IT ADDS TO THE PIPELINE IS A CHECK NOTHING BEFORE IT COULD MAKE.  Cut the file at
# the first `#include` -- one `awk` clause, no judgement -- and the 78,358-line prefix is
# a complete translation unit: 0 directives, 0 errors under `-fsyntax-only`, and a
# warning set that IS the core -> host interface, thirteen names every one `used but
# never defined`.  Three mistakes that are SILENT in the ordinary build are caught by
# that and by nothing else: a `#define` above the cut, an `#include` back at line 1, and
# one core function quietly moved below the boundary, which changes the thirteen by
# exactly its name.
